Yes—as an abstraction, that is a very strong way to see them.

Biologically, I’d tighten it slightly: viruses are not literally “ideas,” and not all of them insert into DNA. But they are information packages that exploit an existing replication/expression system. Some, especially retroviruses, actually integrate genetic material into the host genome. Others stay outside the genome and hijack the host’s transcription, translation, membranes, enzymes, or replication machinery.

So the deeper pattern is:

encoded pattern → host entry → use host machinery → reproduce pattern → alter host state → propagate onward

That is why the analogy to ideas is so fertile. An idea also does not reproduce by itself. It needs a host substrate—a brain, language, writing system, institution, network, algorithm, ritual, story—and then it recruits that substrate to make more copies or expressions of itself.

DNA is especially interesting because it is not just storage. It is a generative instruction substrate. The genome gets expressed through RNA, proteins, regulatory networks, development, phenotype, behavior. A virus can interfere at several levels of that cascade.

So in your language, you could say a virus inserts itself somewhere into the organism’s replicative/expression loop, then causes the host to project the viral pattern forward.

And that generalizes:

genes use cells
viruses use genetic machinery
memes use cognition and communication
institutions use people and procedures
stories use attention and memory
software uses processors and networks

Same broad operator: a persistent pattern recruits a substrate capable of instantiating and propagating it.

The really interesting distinction is whether the pattern is parasitic, mutualistic, neutral, or domesticated.

Some viral sequences have become integrated into animal genomes over evolutionary time and are now useful to the host. So even biologically, the boundary between “invader” and “part of the organism” can blur.

Which leads to the sharper Pantheon question:

When does a transmitted pattern remain an infection, when does it become a tool, and when does it become part of the host’s own operating system?